# shell: desktop
# home: fixture
# prefs: factory
# The whole road, end to end: an application asks the SHELL for a file,
# the person picks one in the shell's own panel, and the application is
# told -- some frames later, over the same session its window rides on.
#
# The editor never learns to walk a filesystem and never draws a panel.
# And because the name it is handed ends in .bas, the document arrives
# coloured: the language is read from the name the shell gave it.
wait 14
note the fixture really planted the program the panel will offer, so a
note missing one reads as a missing FILE rather than a broken road
expect-file /tmp/trk_home/hello.bas
click 980 30
wait 4
menu-pick Applications
wait 4
menu-pick Editor
wait 90
expect-title Edit
note the editor offers Open only because this host can ask for one
click 60 8
wait 6
expect-menu Open…
menu-pick Open…
wait 30
note the SHELL's panel, over the namespace the shell already holds
expect-title Open
note a cancelled panel answers nothing -- and the shell FORGETS the
note ask.  It did not: the note outlived the question, and one Escape
note here refused every application's every ask from then on
key Escape
wait 15
expect-no-title Open
click-window Edit
wait 4
click 60 8
wait 6
menu-pick Open…
wait 30
expect-title Open
note picking the program and pressing Open answers the application
click 400 200
wait 4
click 675 413
wait 40
note the panel is gone and the editor is showing what it was handed
expect-no-title Open
expect-title Edit
note and it arrived COLOURED, because .bas said which language it is:
note the line number receded, the statement in the reserved-word ink
expect-pixel 255 145 838383
expect-pixel 287 145 0000b0
note put the editor away rather than leaving it standing
click-window Edit
wait 6
key Escape
wait 20
expect-no-title Edit
quit
